1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,219
The Alarm Clock

2
00:00:04,219 --> 00:00:09,099
Ha! It didn't ring again!

3
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:12,179
Nolik, let's go fix the alarm clock.

4
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:14,179
Simka, wake Tom Thomas up.

5
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:19,240
Tom Thomas, get up!

6
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:21,280
Tom Thomas!

7
00:00:22,859 --> 00:00:25,000
Hey, you'll be late for school!

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,120
Tom Thomas, get up already!

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,399
This is really something!

10
00:00:35,039 --> 00:00:36,799
And where's the battery in here?

11
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:39,560
No, this is an old mechanical alarm clock.

12
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:41,280
It doesn't work with a battery.

13
00:00:41,399 --> 00:00:42,719
It uses a spring.

14
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:43,920
How's that work?

15
00:00:44,039 --> 00:00:46,200
People wind up the spring tightly,

16
00:00:46,359 --> 00:00:48,439
and then as it slowly unwinds,

17
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:52,439
it turns the gears which turn the hands of the clock.

18
00:00:57,799 --> 00:00:59,840
The alarm clock broke! Let's hurry!

19
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:01,080
and get you washed up!

20
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,159
Tom Thomas, are you just getting up?

21
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:10,000
Dad, the alarm clock didn't go off, it's broken!

22
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:11,960
Here's the problem.

23
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:14,920
It won't turn because the feather is stuck in the gears.

24
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:16,080
Nolik, help me!

25
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:25,000
Papus! What's going on?

26
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,359
It looks like it's an earthquake!

27
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:31,159
It really is broken.

28
00:01:31,719 --> 00:01:34,040
Bad time to throw out this old piece of junk.

29
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:38,439
Tom Thomas, I'm off to work.

30
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:39,640
Don't be late for school.

31
00:01:39,719 --> 00:01:40,520
I won't.

32
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:45,240
Where are we?

33
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:51,320
In the trash can!

34
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:53,520
And what's gonna happen to us?

35
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:55,400
Well, you see, Nolik…

36
00:01:55,400 --> 00:02:02,440
People throw out broken things without a second thought.

37
00:02:03,060 --> 00:02:07,880
Even appliances that can still be fixed end up in the trash all the time.

38
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:13,620
And the trash is taken to a horrible, deadly place called the dump.

39
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:19,580
If a fixie happens to be inside a broken appliance, he will come face to face with great danger.

40
00:02:20,259 --> 00:02:23,740
Once, my uncle got thrown into the dump buried inside an old TV.

41
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:30,740
He barely managed to jump out of the bulldozer's path, and it was a miracle he didn't end up in the incinerator.

42
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:36,680
After that, he just roamed around the huge dump, trying to fix anything.

43
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,840
He became totally crazed.

44
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:45,900
Good thing the Fixie Rescue Squad managed to find him and bring him back home.

45
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:53,939
I don't want to scare you, but we might be taken to the dump, my boy.

46
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:56,020
Papus, I'm scared.

47
00:02:57,180 --> 00:02:59,640
Huh, where is the alarm clock?

48
00:02:59,759 --> 00:03:01,700
Maybe my dad took it to get fixed.

49
00:03:02,099 --> 00:03:04,580
But Nolik and Papus are in there.

50
00:03:05,259 --> 00:03:07,159
Now just a little bit further.

51
00:03:08,879 --> 00:03:10,780
I don't want to go to the dump.

52
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:13,460
No tears. There's only one way out of here.

53
00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:16,060
We need to fix the clock and make it ring!

54
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:17,460
But how?

55
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:25,860
Inside the clock there is the main spring and there's also a second spring.

56
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:29,860
The second spring is held still by a brake and so it waits.

57
00:03:30,659 --> 00:03:33,860
When the little hand reaches the time the alarm was set to go off,

58
00:03:34,260 --> 00:03:38,860
the spring jumps off the brake and the gears are free to start turning.

59
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:43,660
That makes a little hammer beat the cup of a bell very, very quickly.

60
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:48,180
And that's how an alarm clock rings.

61
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:56,460
So this feather is stopping the gears and not letting the hammer strike the bell.

62
00:03:56,820 --> 00:03:57,820
Exactly!

63
00:03:57,980 --> 00:04:00,460
I'll start rocking the gear back and forth and you tug it.

64
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:07,860
And one, and two, and three!

65
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:09,100
Tideesh!

66
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:18,420
Simka, I think I can hear my alarm ringing.

67
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:20,540
Run to the sound! Quickly!

68
00:04:23,139 --> 00:04:25,980
Uh-huh! Someone turn the alarm off!

69
00:04:26,699 --> 00:04:29,980
Wow! And here comes that earthquake again!

70
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:36,220
Nolik! Nolik! I'm here!

71
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:38,060
No, Leg.

72
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:39,660
We fixed the alarm clock!

73
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:41,260
So what was wrong with it?

74
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:43,580
A feather got jammed in the gears.

75
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:46,380
And how could a feather get in a clock?

76
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:50,260
Oh, it's probably from when I put the alarm clock under my pillow,

77
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:51,620
so it wouldn't wake me up.

78
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:56,300
So you mean because somebody doesn't like to get up in the morning,

79
00:04:56,620 --> 00:04:58,660
we almost ended up at the dump?

80
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:04,260
By the way, if that somebody doesn't hurry off to his school soon, he'll be late!

81
00:05:04,340 --> 00:05:06,060
Oh, you're right!

82
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:07,060
Come on.

